        "titulo": "Review: participles and time markers",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: irregular participles: «fatto, detto, preso, visto, letto, scritto, messo, aperto, chiesto, risposto, scelto, perso, vinto, bevuto». STEP 2: after «avere» the participle does not agree with the subject (it changes only for a direct object pronoun such as «lo, la, li, le», in lesson 23): «io ho letto», «lei ha letto», «noi abbiamo letto» (the helper changes, but the participle does not agree with the subject). STEP 3: verbs like «finire», «cominciare», «cambiare» take «avere» with a direct object and «essere» without one. STEP 4: «fa» goes after the amount of time, «scorso» goes after the noun, and «da» + present says how long something has lasted.",
        "comparacao": "Compare each verb in English with its Italian pair: «read, read, read», «see, saw, seen», «write, wrote, written». The English forms are also irregular, so you already know the idea. Practice the Italian pair as a single unit and the sentence will come out right.",
